4. End-to-End Workflow
Step 1 — Developer Creates a Commit
The developer runs git commit, which invokes .githooks/pre-commit.
Step 2 — Detect Staged C# Changes
The harness identifies only staged C# files. If there are no staged .cs files, the Sonar validation is skipped.
Step 3 — Identify Affected Projects
For each staged C# file, the harness determines the owning .csproj and limits analysis to affected projects.
Step 4 — Run Roslyn/Sonar Analysis
Each affected project is built with analyzers enabled and SARIF output generated.
-p:RunAnalyzers=true -p:ErrorLog=<sarif-file>
Step 5 — Merge and Filter SARIF Results
Results are merged into .git/sonar-harness/merged.sarif. Blocking issues are Sonar S* rules at error severity affecting staged files.
.git/sonar-harness/sonar-summary.json
Step 6 — SonarQube Metadata Enrichment
When blocking Sonar issues are detected, the harness queries SonarQube to enrich them with rule details such as the rule description, root cause, and recommended fix strategy. The enriched results are stored in sonar-failure.json.
Step 6 — Copilot-Driven Remediation
The harness passes the enriched Sonar issues to a VS Code Copilot Agent through a structured remediation prompt. The Agent analyzes the violations, applies appropriate fixes, validates the changes, and presents the proposed remediation to the developer.
Step 6 —Human-in-the-Loop Approval
The Agent cannot make changes without explicit developer approval:
•	ACCEPT_AND_PROCEED — stages the changes and restarts git commit, triggering the Sonar gate again. 
•	REJECT_AND_RETRY — allows the Agent to revise the remediation and try again. 
ACCEPT → git add -A → git commit → Pre-commit validation
REJECT → Copilot remediation retry
9. Generated Artifacts
All harness-generated artifacts are stored under:
.git/sonar-harness/
Generated artifacts remain outside normal repository history.
10. Core Components
Component	File	Responsibility
Git Hook Entry Point	.githooks/pre-commit	Entry point for the commit workflow. Invokes analyzer, enrichment, and remediation components.
Analyzer Gate	tools/sonar-precommit.ps1	Detects staged C# files, resolves affected projects, runs dotnet build with analyzers, generates SARIF, filters Sonar S* rules as errors, and determines whether the commit is blocked.
Metadata Enrichment	tools/sonar-enrich.ps1	Queries SonarQube for rule information such as root cause and remediation guidance.
Remediation Launcher	tools/sonar-remediate.ps1	Generates the Copilot remediation prompt, stores it in history, and optionally launches VS Code.
Commit Restart Helper	tools/sonar-remediation-proceed.ps1	Stages Agent changes and restarts git commit so the pre-commit validation runs again.
Analyzer Configuration	Directory.Build.props	Provides shared build/analyzer configuration used by affected .NET projects.
11. Design Principles
Fail Fast and Locally: Detect Sonar violations at commit time rather than after code reaches CI.
Analyze Only What Changed: Scope validation to staged .cs files and their associated projects.
Reuse Existing Analyzer Configuration: Use the existing SonarAnalyzer.CSharp configuration referenced by the .NET projects.
Keep SonarQube Enrichment Optional: Use SonarQube for additional context without making it a hard dependency for the local gate.
Human-in-the-Loop AI: Use Copilot as a remediation assistant while requiring explicit developer approval.
Revalidate AI-Generated Changes: Restart git commit after remediation so the same pre-commit gate validates the changes.
Keep Generated Artifacts Ephemeral: Store generated SARIF, metadata, and prompts under .git/sonar-harness/.
Cross-Shell Compatibility: Use a POSIX sh hook that tries pwsh first and falls back to powershell.exe.
12. How to set up
Step 1 — Download the harness code
Download the ZIP file (Sonar Harness) and extract the content inside the root folder of your C#/.Net project.
Step 1 — Configure Git Hooks
git config core.hooksPath .githooks
git config --get core.hooksPath
Expected output: .githooks
Step 2 — Configure SonarQube Enrichment
1.	Sonar Enrichment Configuration (Recommended)
a.	Configure following User environment variables:
i.	SONAR_HOST_URL 
ii.	SONAR_TOKEN: <refer below>
iii.	SONAR_PROJECT_KEY: <refer below>
2.	How to get SONAR_TOKEN:
a.	Sign in to your SonarQube server in the browser.
b.	Open your profile or account settings.
c.	Go to Security.
d.	Create a new token.
e.	Copy the token value immediately and store it securely.
3.	How to get SONAR_PROJECT_KEY:
a.	Sign in to SonarQube.
b.	Open your project.
c.	From the left navigation, go to Project Information.
d.	Under About this Project, you will be able to find the Project Key.
Step 3 — Verify .NET
dotnet --version
dotnet build
Step 4 — Verify VS Code CLI
code --version
13. Verification
1.	Make a C# change that violates a configured Sonar rule.
2.	Stage the change using git add.
3.	Attempt to commit using git commit.
4.	Verify that the commit is blocked.
5.	Check sonar-summary.json and sonar-failure.json under .git/sonar-harness/.
6.	Verify that a VS Code Copilot Agent session is launched when automatic remediation is enabled.
7.	Review the proposed changes.
8.	Select ACCEPT_AND_PROCEED.
9.	Verify that the commit is restarted.
10.	Confirm that the pre-commit Sonar gate executes again.
11.	Verify that the commit succeeds once all blocking violations are resolved.
16. Summary
The Sonar Harness turns Sonar analysis into a developer-local, pre-commit quality gate rather than relying exclusively on CI-based validation.
Native Git Hooks + Roslyn/SonarAnalyzer.CSharp + SonarQube Rule Enrichment + GitHub Copilot Agent + Human Approval + Automatic Revalidation
The resulting workflow is: Detect Sonar issues → Understand the issue → Generate a fix → Review the fix → Re-run Sonar analysis → Commit only when the gate passes.
